WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.633
Requirements begin with stakeholders who have
needs, constraints and competing priorities.

00:00:05.633 --> 00:00:10.765
Elicitation gathers these concerns; analysis
examines consistency, feasibility and

00:00:10.765 --> 00:00:16.336
completeness; specification records an agreed
account; validation checks that the account

00:00:16.336 --> 00:00:18.151
addresses the actual problem.

00:00:18.151 --> 00:00:23.308
A request to use a particular technology may
reflect a real operational constraint, or it

00:00:23.308 --> 00:00:26.669
may be a proposed solution that hides the
underlying need.

00:00:26.669 --> 00:00:31.819
Ask why a statement matters and who is affected
if it is unmet. Distinguish a business goal

00:00:31.819 --> 00:00:36.855
from an observable system behavior and from a
contractual or organizational constraint. A

00:00:36.855 --> 00:00:40.307
system boundary helps expose missing actors and
dependencies.

00:00:40.307 --> 00:00:45.242
Conflicts should become explicit tradeoffs
rather than being concealed in vague wording.

00:00:45.242 --> 00:00:49.953
Architecture then starts from a clearer problem,
avoiding detailed optimization of a

00:00:49.953 --> 00:00:54.383
solution that stakeholders never needed. A
stakeholder says “use Java.” Is that

00:00:54.383 --> 00:00:56.570
automatically a functional requirement?

00:00:56.807 --> 00:01:02.456
No. It is a technology constraint or proposed
solution; discover its rationale and whether

00:01:02.456 --> 00:01:04.025
it is actually mandatory.

00:01:04.025 --> 00:01:09.482
A specification bridges stakeholder needs and
implementation. It may describe functions,

00:01:09.482 --> 00:01:15.188
data, interfaces, quality constraints, operating
environments and delivery obligations. Good

00:01:15.188 --> 00:01:20.894
requirements are understandable, consistent,
verifiable and traceable to goals. Completeness

00:01:20.894 --> 00:01:26.166
does not mean unlimited detail; it means
including what is necessary for the intended

00:01:26.166 --> 00:01:30.507
agreement and identifying unresolved questions
instead of hiding them.

00:01:30.525 --> 00:01:34.969
Ambiguity becomes visible when different readers
can produce different valid

00:01:34.969 --> 00:01:40.349
interpretations. Soup or salad and bread has
more than one grouping; a name that “can be 127

00:01:40.349 --> 00:01:45.729
characters” leaves optionality and length
boundaries unclear. Rewrite statements so a
tester

00:01:45.729 --> 00:01:48.419
can distinguish compliance from noncompliance.

00:01:48.525 --> 00:01:53.455
Preserve links to rationale and design choices,
because changing a requirement after

00:01:53.455 --> 00:01:58.679
deployment may affect architecture, tests,
training and operational procedures as well as

00:01:58.679 --> 00:02:03.668
code. Can two reviewers agree that a sentence
sounds reasonable yet disagree about an

00:02:03.668 --> 00:02:05.194
implementation passing it?

00:02:05.194 --> 00:02:10.518
Yes. Missing boundaries, definitions and
acceptance criteria allow incompatible

00:02:10.518 --> 00:02:14.022
interpretations; testable examples help expose
them.

00:02:14.194 --> 00:02:19.609
Functional requirements describe what the system
does, while quality requirements constrain

00:02:19.609 --> 00:02:24.429
how well relevant behavior occurs under stated
conditions. Delivery or technology

00:02:24.429 --> 00:02:29.487
constraints form additional categories. These
categories do not determine priority: a

00:02:29.487 --> 00:02:34.605
quality requirement can be essential, and a
proposed function can be optional. “Secure

00:02:34.605 --> 00:02:39.842
login” needs decomposition into authentication
behavior and explicit security properties

00:02:39.842 --> 00:02:41.805
rather than a stronger adjective.

00:02:41.805 --> 00:02:46.878
Quality attributes often span multiple
responsibilities. Reliability can depend on

00:02:46.878 --> 00:02:52.259
persistence, retries, status reporting and
recovery; performance can depend on database

00:02:52.259 --> 00:02:57.702
work, communication and workload. They therefore
influence architecture, even though the

00:02:57.702 --> 00:03:00.981
system can continue evolving after an initial
design.

00:03:00.981 --> 00:03:05.831
Express these concerns in scenarios and
acceptance checks. Asking how an attribute is

00:03:05.831 --> 00:03:10.853
observed makes it easier to compare designs and
prevents quality from becoming a list of

00:03:10.853 --> 00:03:15.303
aspirations. Is a two-second response
requirement less mandatory because it is

00:03:15.303 --> 00:03:16.102
nonfunctional?

00:03:16.102 --> 00:03:21.207
No. Mandatory status depends on the agreement
and system context, not whether the

00:03:21.207 --> 00:03:24.736
requirement describes a function or a quality
attribute.
